# The two ports this app listens on, and the only place either number is
# written. `ports.ts` beside this file reads them for `vite.config.ts` and
# `playwright.config.ts`; `turbo/generators/config.ts` rewrites these two lines
# when it clones the app, so a generated app runs beside the Template it came
# from.
#
# This Runtime reads the file from BOTH directions, which is why it carries the
# same shape as the other two. `react-router dev` is a Vite dev server, so it
# takes `server.port` out of `vite.config.ts` — the `DEV_PORT` export of
# `ports.ts`. `react-router-serve` is an Express process with no config file at
# all: the only channel into it is `PORT`, so the `start` script hands this file
# to dotenv-cli exactly as the Next Template does.
#
# Not app config. It sits outside Vite's `envDir` ("../../") and is not named
# `.env*`, so no bundler ever loads it — app config is still the repo-root
# `.env` (ADR-0003).
#
# Ports are allocated one pair per app — dev 3000+n, e2e 3100+n. `apps/storybook`
# is deliberately outside the bands on Storybook's own 6006 and has no E2E
# server, so it declares no `ports.env`.
PORT=3005
# One hundred above the dev port, so a forgotten `bun run dev` can never answer
# an E2E run: `reuseExistingServer` would hand those specs the dev server's
# module graph and the run would still look green. It matters more here than
# anywhere else — `react-router-serve` with an empty `PORT` picks the first free
# port it finds rather than failing, so a run that lands on the wrong server is
# a real possibility rather than a theoretical one.
E2E_PORT=3105
